      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The aim of this study was to investigate the plasma adipokine responses to high-intensity interval training (HIT) in overweight/obese women. Twelve women (age 21.7\u2009±\u20093.8 years) completed a 19 days of HIT comprising six session of 4-6 repeats of a Wingate test (0.065\u2009kg load/kg). Plasma adipokine levels were measured before exercise, and at 5 and 90\u2009min after exercise on the first and the last training days. Adiponectin was higher at 5\u2009min than 90\u2009min post-exercise (11.7\u2009±\u20097.3 and 10.5\u2009±\u20095.8\u2009ng/ml; p\u2009=\u2009.01) in the first exercise day. Leptin decreased 5\u2009min after exercise (23.6\u2009±\u200913.2 vs. baseline 27.8\u2009±\u200914.4\u2009ng/ml; p\u2009<\u2009.01) and remained depressed following 90\u2009min (p\u2009<\u2009.01). The changes in adiponectin and leptin concentrations were similar on the first and last exercise days. No consistent effect was found on resistin concentration. Future studies are required to disclose the functional consequences of these alterations in plasma adipokine levels."},
